\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.10}{\ignorespaces Sketches of the WS representation for a Guassian beam interacting with different parts of a simple optical layout consisting of two active lenses with powers $S_{1,2} + \Delta S_{1,2}$. The left hand axis shows the WS plane at lens 1. The input beam $q_1$ is indicated by a red dot, and the effect of the lens on $q_1$ is shown by the dark green arrow $S_1$. This results in a new mode $q_2$, which is shown as a blue dot. A light green arrow indicates the actuation of lens 1. The right hand axis shows the WS plane at lens 2. The input mode at lens 2, $q_3$, is shown as a purple dot, and the the output mode , $q_4$, is shown as a pink dot. The static and dynamic power of lens 2 is indicated by the orange and yellow lines. As lens 1 and lens 2 are separated by $45\si {\degree }$ of Gouy Phase, the actuation provided by lens 1 results in a change in the beam parameter which is orthogonal to the actuation provide by lens 2, provided that the actuations are small enough. \relax }}{34}{figure.caption.46}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterwssketch}{{1.10}{34}{Sketches of the WS representation for a Guassian beam interacting with different parts of a simple optical layout consisting of two active lenses with powers $S_{1,2} + \Delta S_{1,2}$. The left hand axis shows the WS plane at lens 1. The input beam $q_1$ is indicated by a red dot, and the effect of the lens on $q_1$ is shown by the dark green arrow $S_1$. This results in a new mode $q_2$, which is shown as a blue dot. A light green arrow indicates the actuation of lens 1. The right hand axis shows the WS plane at lens 2. The input mode at lens 2, $q_3$, is shown as a purple dot, and the the output mode , $q_4$, is shown as a pink dot. The static and dynamic power of lens 2 is indicated by the orange and yellow lines. As lens 1 and lens 2 are separated by $45\si {\degree }$ of Gouy Phase, the actuation provided by lens 1 results in a change in the beam parameter which is orthogonal to the actuation provide by lens 2, provided that the actuations are small enough. \relax }{figure.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces \relax }}{36}{figure.caption.48}}
\newlabel{}{{1.11}{36}{\relax }{figure.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces Top panel: This is the WS space at the OMx2 plane. After reflection from OMx2, a mode must be at least $98\%$ mode matched to the waist as there are no further active mode matching elements in the layout of HAM6. The target modes are shown by the red region. The blue region represents which modes can be reached from the red region by either $S_{\rm  {OMx2}}\pm \Delta S$, and was constructed by applying transformations to the modes in the red region. The red region was split into two parts, `upper' and `lower', which approximately corresponds to modes with a defocus greater than 0.6 or less than 0.6. The line that splits the red area into an upper and lower region corresponds to the line that connects the two extremes in width of the region, and does not have to be a horizontal line. To create the lower edge of the blue region, modes in the lower edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm  {OMx2}}-\Delta S$ applied to them. To construct the upper edge of the blue region, upper edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm  {OMx2}}+\Delta S$ applied to them. These regions were then joined by a vertical line that connects them.  Bottom panel: This is the WS space at the OMx1 plane. The pink mode represent the target modes that a mode must be able to reach by being reflected from OMx1. The blue region from the upper panel was propagated back to OMx1 to form the pink region. In a similar way as before, the pink region was split into an upper and lower region, and the green region was constructed from this. Modes in the green region are capable of being transformed to the pink region, which therefore means thy can be transformed to the red region, meaning they satisfy the $98\%$ mode matching requirement. The green region was propagated back to the through the layout to SRM, and this is as the purple region in Figure \ref  {fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5fmodessrmomcmodematchingsuccess}.\relax }}{37}{figure.caption.49}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5fomx1omx2planes}{{1.11}{37}{Top panel: This is the WS space at the OMx2 plane. After reflection from OMx2, a mode must be at least $98\%$ mode matched to the waist as there are no further active mode matching elements in the layout of HAM6. The target modes are shown by the red region. The blue region represents which modes can be reached from the red region by either $S_{\rm {OMx2}}\pm \Delta S$, and was constructed by applying transformations to the modes in the red region. The red region was split into two parts, `upper' and `lower', which approximately corresponds to modes with a defocus greater than 0.6 or less than 0.6. The line that splits the red area into an upper and lower region corresponds to the line that connects the two extremes in width of the region, and does not have to be a horizontal line. To create the lower edge of the blue region, modes in the lower edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm {OMx2}}-\Delta S$ applied to them. To construct the upper edge of the blue region, upper edge of the red region had the transformation corresponding to a mirror power of $S_{\rm {OMx2}}+\Delta S$ applied to them. These regions were then joined by a vertical line that connects them.\\ Bottom panel: This is the WS space at the OMx1 plane. The pink mode represent the target modes that a mode must be able to reach by being reflected from OMx1. The blue region from the upper panel was propagated back to OMx1 to form the pink region. In a similar way as before, the pink region was split into an upper and lower region, and the green region was constructed from this. Modes in the green region are capable of being transformed to the pink region, which therefore means thy can be transformed to the red region, meaning they satisfy the $98\%$ mode matching requirement. The green region was propagated back to the through the layout to SRM, and this is as the purple region in Figure \ref {fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5fmodessrmomcmodematchingsuccess}.\relax }{figure.caption.49}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.12}{\ignorespaces \relax }}{38}{figure.caption.50}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.12}{\ignorespaces Top panel: the set of modes that could be present at the SRM is shown by the purple-blue-green-yellow curve, as described in chapter \ref  {chapter:simulation_finesse}. The purple region shows which modes can reach 98\% mode matching by using the the AWC in HAM6. The orange cross indicates the mode that corresponds to the SRC having a single pass Gouy phase of 21.3\si {\degree }. The contours indicate the mode matching between the arm mode and the OMC mode. Bottom panel: this shows the same modes as the top panel, except they have been transformed to the plane of the OMC waist. The target region is highlighted with a hatched area.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.51}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5fmodessrmomcmodematchingsuccess}{{1.12}{39}{Top panel: the set of modes that could be present at the SRM is shown by the purple-blue-green-yellow curve, as described in chapter \ref {chapter:simulation_finesse}. The purple region shows which modes can reach 98\% mode matching by using the the AWC in HAM6. The orange cross indicates the mode that corresponds to the SRC having a single pass Gouy phase of 21.3\si {\degree }. The contours indicate the mode matching between the arm mode and the OMC mode. Bottom panel: this shows the same modes as the top panel, except they have been transformed to the plane of the OMC waist. The target region is highlighted with a hatched area.\relax }{figure.caption.51}{}}
